{
  "rules": [
    "When recent history shows a downtrend (e.g., from ~72 to ~61) followed by a model forecast that assumes immediate recovery or linear growth, apply a strong downward correction bias of 5-8% for the first 10 days.",
    "The model consistently overestimates volatility and fails to capture deep mean reversion; true outcomes often drop 10-15 points below the forecasted path during the first two weeks after a local low.",
    "Sentiment history showing a single -1.00 spike followed by neutral values (0.00) indicates a temporary shock that the model ignores; correct forecasts by reducing the expected growth rate by 20-30% until sentiment stabilizes.",
    "The model exhibits significant lag in recognizing trend reversals; when the last 5 days of history are below the forecast baseline, assume the next 15 days will be lower than predicted before any recovery occurs.",
    "Avoid linear extrapolation from a single data point (e.g., using the most recent close as the start); instead, anchor forecasts to the median of the last 7 days adjusted for a negative drift factor when sentiment is neutral or slightly positive."
  ]
}
